<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pinsk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pinsk is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-france">Brest</a> region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, where the Pina joins the Pripyat, in the heart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pripet-marshes">Pinsk Marshes</a>. First recorded in 1097, it was a town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turov">Turov</a> lands and later a Lithuanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> duchy. In modern times it had a large Jewish community, most of whom were murdered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pinsk.jpg" alt="Pinsk" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Liilia Moroz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pinsk_Lenina_street.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pinsk lies in Polesia at the confluence of the Pina and the Pripyat, in a region once known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pripet-marshes">Pinsk Marshes</a>. Nestor the Chronicler first mentioned it in 1097. In the High Middle Ages it passed between various principalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turov">Turov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. The Mongol invasion destroyed it in 1240, and in 1241 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> diocese moved to Pinsk from Turov. By 1320 it was part of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, under local lines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminids">Gediminids</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the local princely line died out in 1521, Pinsk became a royal possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I the Old</a>, who gave it in 1523 to his wife, Queen Bona Sforza. She had a canal built and the city flourished. King Stephen Báthory granted it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> rights and its coat of arms in 1581, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> founded a monastery and college there in 1631. In 1648 local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> residents let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> into the city, and the rebels massacred Catholic clergy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. Russians and Cossacks attacked again in 1655, and in 1657 Cossacks destroyed the city. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition of Poland</a> brought Pinsk under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1793.</p><p>A railway line arrived in 1882, and Pinsk became an important commercial centre. According to the Russian census of 1897, 21,100 of its 28,400 inhabitants were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, 74 per cent, making it one of the most Jewish cities under tsarist rule. It changed hands several times between 1919 and 1920 and became part of the Second Polish Republic under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-riga">Peace of Riga</a>. In April 1919 the Polish Army executed 35 Jewish civilians accused of collaborating with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, which created a diplomatic crisis noted at the Versailles Conference.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> occupied Pinsk in September 1939. German forces took it on 4 July 1941, and a ghetto was established in 1942. Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were killed in late October 1942 when the ghetto was liquidated, with 10,000 murdered in one day. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> retook the city on 14 July 1944, just under 6,000 people remained. Pinsk became part of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> in 1991. Its landmarks include the Assumption Cathedral of the Monastery of the Greyfriars, built from 1712 to 1730, and the Butrymowicz Palace of 1784 to 1790.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pinsk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Over time it split into smaller appanages based on Turov, <b>Pinsk</b> and other towns, and its princes became minor players.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-turov-and-pinsk">Principality of Turov and Pinsk</a></li></ul></section>
